unicorn --> ref`}),e.jsx(n,{children:"Meaning"}),e.jsxs(t,{children:["A ",e.jsxs(s,{children:[e.jsx(a,{}),"referent"]})," is anything that can be picked out, pointed to, or distinguished—anything that can be the target of reference. The term captures the most general category of existence within any system of meaning. If something can be mentioned, if it can be the object of thought or discourse, if it can be indicated in any way, then it is a referent."]}),e.jsx(t,{children:'The concept of "referent" provides the minimal case—what anything must be to exist within meaningful discourse. A referent, at minimum, must be distinguishable as itself, must be this rather than that. This is why the canonical referent—the abstract notion of "a referent"—demonstrates only self-equality as its essential property. It has no other characteristics, no other relationships, no properties beyond being itself. It is pure distinguishability without further qualification.'}),e.jsx(t,{children:"Referents include everything we can speak or think about: concrete objects (mountains, molecules), abstract objects (numbers, virtues), fictional entities (unicorns, Hamlet), impossible objects (square circles, the largest number), experiences (pains, memories), and even absences (the gap in the fence, the silence after the music). The breadth of referenthood reflects the breadth of meaningful discourse itself."}),e.jsx(t,{children:"Crucially, being a referent makes no claims about any other form of existence. To say something is a referent is not to say it exists physically, or mentally, or platonically, or in any particular way. It is only to say it can be referred to. Fictional characters are referents—we refer to them constantly. Past events that have ceased to exist are referents. Future possibilities that may never occur are referents. Dreams, hallucinations, mistakes—all are referents within the space of meaning."}),e.jsx(t,{children:`This generality makes "referent" the ultimate category, the horizon within which all other distinctions are made. When we debate what exists, we're not debating what counts as a referent—the mere fact of being debatable already establishes that. We're debating what properties different referents possess, what relationships they enter into, how they're organized within the total space of meaning.`}),e.jsx(n,{children:"6. Symbol"}),e.jsx(n,{children:"Formal Facts"}),e.jsx(r,{language:"srt",children:`sym =sym> lit
